parts.text:
The anti-slop lint rule complains about the explicit open dictionary type `Record<EmailKind, (props: PlaceholderProps) => ReactElement>` — "discards known type evidence. Keep inference, validate with `satisfies`, or use a named owner contract."

Fix: declare the object without the explicit type annotation, then validate with `satisfies`:

```tsx
const RENDERERS = {
  inviteMember: (p) => ...,
  ...
} satisfies Record<EmailKind, (props: PlaceholderProps) => ReactElement>;
```

But then parameter types in lambdas become implicit any... With `satisfies`, contextual typing doesn't flow to parameters before checking? Actually `satisfies` DOES provide contextual typing for object literal expressions — TypeScript uses the target type as contextual type for the expression when using satisfies. Yes: `const x = { a: (p) => p } satisfies Record<string, (p: number) => number>` contextually types p as number. Confirmed behavior since TS 4.9.

So rewrite with satisfies and keep lambda params implicitly typed via context.

